prompt: "Exaplain and demonstrate some of the features in drawcast (see drawcast.app) #long #podcast"

## A drawcast, drawing itself
use: cell_diagram
with:
    organelles:
      - nucleus
      - mitochondria
      - er
      - golgi
      - ribosomes
    labels: true

A: If you've ever tried to animate a teaching figure, you know the pain. Drawcast makes it easy.
    text heading "A drawcast, drawing itself" x 500 y 715 font_size 34

    ask "What should I call you?" store name default friend
        intro: Before anything gets drawn.

B: Hi, {name} — good to have you. You can answer, click, and poke at every figure here; this is not a video pretending to be a lesson.

A: So let's explain something that isn't maths at all: how a cell works. The double line is the membrane, the gatekeeper that decides what gets in.
    draw cytoplasm membrane membrane_inner

B: The nucleus holds the DNA — the recipe library. Nothing leaves it whole; only copies of single recipes go out.
    draw nucleus nucleus_fill nucleolus label_nucleus

A: These dots are ribosomes, and they read a copy and build the protein it describes, one amino acid at a time.
    draw ribosomes label_ribo

B: The folded ribbons are the endoplasmic reticulum, where new proteins are shaped, and the stack is the Golgi, where they get packed and addressed.
    draw er label_er golgi label_golgi

A: And this one burns sugar with oxygen to pay for all of it — the mitochondrion, its inner folds cramming more working surface into the same space.
    draw mito cristae label_mito

B: It also carries its own small ring of DNA, inherited only from your mother — a leftover from the day a bacterium was swallowed and never digested.
    focus mito cristae delivery soft

A: Two sets of genes in one cell, then — and notice what the drawing did while we talked: each part arrived on the sentence that explained it.
    highlight nucleus mito effect glow

    quiz "Which organelle carries its own DNA?" intro "Quick check."
        right: The mitochondrion — its own ring of DNA, passed down the maternal line.
        wrong: Not that one — think of the part descended from a swallowed bacterium.
        * The Golgi apparatus
        + The mitochondrion
        * The ribosome

## When the numbers change
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Ash
      - Brook
      - Cole
      - Dana
    values:
      - [12, 31, 22, 8]
      - [44, 35, 51, 19]
    stage: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 60
    y_label: Votes (thousands)
    value_labels: true
    title: Election night (invented numbers)

A: Watch this one, {name}. Four candidates, one invented election night — the numbers are made up, the mechanism is not.
    draw title

    draw axes

B: At nine in the evening, the early boxes are counted and Brook is comfortably ahead.
    draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 parallel true

A: Thirty-one thousand against Cole's twenty-two — the kind of lead a newsroom calls decisive.
    point at.ref bar_2 gesture circle

B: Then the city boxes arrive, and the bars simply follow the new numbers — no keyframes, no redrawing.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

A: Cole ends the night in front. You hand over the numbers for each stage, and this is the same machinery behind a bar-chart race — the bars even reorder as they overtake.
    highlight bar_3 effect glow

    pause 0.4

B: Which means a chart here is not a picture of data: it is the data, drawn.

## Numbers that move, shapes that argue
vars: {"f":1}

A: Now the part that earns the bet: what happens when one number changes.
    text title "Numbers that move, shapes that argue" x 500 y 715 font_size 34

B: The paper is a thousand units across and seven hundred and fifty up, with zero-zero bottom left — a maths plot, not screen pixels.
    dot dot_center #b5482e at.x 500 at.y 280
    label label_center "The middle" left #b5482e attach_to dot_center

A: And that is nearly the last coordinate you'll hear, because almost everything hangs off something else instead.
    erase dot_center label_center

    axes ax x_label x y_label y

B: This curve is a formula — sine of f times x — and f is one number the whole page shares, printed there as f equals one.
    curve wave #2f6b8f expr "50 + 30*sin(f*0.0628*x)"
    text readout "f = {f:1}" #8a5fa8 x 800 y 650 font_size 28

A: And this point is defined, not placed: x is twenty-five, its height read off the wave wherever the wave happens to be.
    dot dot_wave #b5482e at.on wave at.x 25
    label label_dot "x = 25" above #b5482e attach_to dot_wave

B: Push f from one to four and the curve, the printed number and the point all recompute together — nobody keyframed a single position.
    animate f 4 duration 6 easing linear

A: The camera can lean into a detail too, then step back out.
    camera center.ref dot_wave zoom 2

    camera reset true

B: Which lets you do something older than any of this: an argument made of moving pieces, the way Archimedes' heirs did it with paper.
    clear keep ["title"]

A: Fourteen equal slices of a circle of radius r — and each slice is its own element, not a stroke in a picture.
    pieces kake #b5482e x 300 y 350 radius 140 n 14 of sectors style.fill #f2c14e

    keep kake

B: Keep a ghost of the circle where it stood, and zip the slices alternately up and down — every position and every turn computed.
    arrange kake layout zipper at [680,360] duration 3

A: The bumpy rectangle is r high and half the circumference long: pi r times r.
    math eq tex "A = \\pi r \\cdot r = \\pi r^2" x 680 y 150

B: Nothing was added or thrown away, only rearranged — and that's the whole idea: you describe the things and the story, and the drawing does the arguing.
    highlight eq effect glow

    quiz "On drawcast's canvas, where is the point (0, 0)?" intro "Quick check."
        right: Bottom-left — y increases upward, like a maths plot.
        wrong: Not quite — it reads like a graph, not like screen pixels.
        * Top-left corner
        + Bottom-left corner
        * The centre
---
